  "name": "RUBELLA - IgG & IgM",
  "code": "2039",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p>Rubella IgG/ IgM : When affecting adults it is usually trivial and benign. However, the disease has serious implications when it occurs in<br>pregnant woman, especially during first trimester of pregnancy. In acute rubella infection IgM specific to rubella is elevated. However,rubella<br>specific IgM antibodies often perisist for 20-30 days after vaccination and can be reliably detected in foetal serum by 22 weeks of<br>pregnancy. In infants with congenital rubella major increases in total IgM are also common in foetal blood. detection of rubella specific IgM<br>is the best way to evaluate rubella antibodies during the first six months life in newborns and in infants with congenital rubella or evidence<br>of prenatal infection. It also assists in diagnosis of acute infection in a non-immune adult.</p>\n<p><strong>LIMITATION OF THE TEST:</strong><br>False positive results can occur in Cross reactivity, Presence of rheumatoid factor, Persistence of IgM antibodies several months after<br>acute infection., Reactivation of virus. False negative results can occur Immunologically immature host.</p>",
